            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Johannes Kepler</strong><br>\nJohannes Kepler was born on December 27, 1571, in the small town of Weil der Stadt in the Holy Roman Empire, present-day Germany. Despite his family's financial struggles, Kepler's intelligence earned him a scholarship to the University of Tübingen. It was here that he first became interested in astronomy, studying under Michael Maestlin, an advocate of the Copernican theory that the Earth orbits the sun.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Kepler's Astronomical Laws</strong><br>\nKepler is renowned for his three laws of planetary motion. His first law states that planets orbit the sun in ellipses with the sun at one focus. His second law, known as the law of equal areas, describes the speed at which any given planet will move while orbiting the sun. The third law establishes a relationship between the time a planet takes to orbit the sun and its distance from the sun.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Work with Tycho Brahe</strong><br>\nIn 1600, Kepler went to Prague to work with Tycho Brahe, the Danish nobleman and astronomer. Brahe had amassed the most precise astronomical data of his time, which Kepler used after Brahe's death. Kepler's analysis of this data led him to formulate his famous laws of planetary motion.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Kepler's Books on Astronomy</strong><br>\nKepler published many books that had a significant impact on astronomy. His \"Astronomia nova\" (\"New Astronomy\") released in 1609, contained his first two laws of planetary motion. Later, his work \"Harmonices Mundi\" (\"Harmony of the Worlds\"), published in 1619, introduced his third law.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Discoveries in Optics</strong><br>\nApart from astronomy, Kepler made discoveries in the field of optics. He explained how vision works, detailing the process of how the eye focuses light and the role of the retina. Kepler's work laid the groundwork for the invention of the telescope and improved the understanding of sight itself.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Challenges and Personal Tragedy</strong><br>\nKepler faced many challenges throughout his life, including religious tension, financial hardships, and personal tragedy. His mother was accused of witchcraft, a charge from which Kepler defended her. He lost his wife and children to illness, which profoundly affected his personal and professional life.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
