          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Relationship with the Church</strong><br>\nAs a devout Lutheran, Kepler's relationship with the church was complex. His support for the Copernican system, which was controversial and opposed by some church authorities, put him at odds with the official doctrine. However, he remained committed to his faith and sought to reconcile science and religion.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Kepler's Mathematical Contributions</strong><br>\nKepler's contributions to mathematics were also substantial. He devised an improved method for calculating the volume of wine barrels, which was just one application of his work in this field. His mathematical precision was crucial to the accuracy of his astronomical predictions and laws.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Death</strong><br>\nJohannes Kepler died on November 15, 1630, in Regensburg. His legacy endures in the fields of astronomy, mathematics, and optics. His laws of planetary motion paved the way for Isaac Newton's theory of gravitation and remain fundamental to our understanding of the solar system's workings.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
